# /etc/binder/binder.env: Binder's settings on the server (root, mode 600).
# setup-server.sh writes it from this file once and never overwrites it.
# Every setting is described in binder/config.py.
BINDER_AUTH=header
BINDER_DB=/var/lib/binder/binder.db
# The sign-in team allowed in (X-Offdesk-Teams); this install's own.
BINDER_TEAM=offdesk
# Who a record can be handled by, as email=Name pairs separated by commas.
# Unset means only "nobody" can be chosen. Restart after a change.
# BINDER_OWNERS=someone@example.com=Someone,other@example.com=Other
# Research and summarise stay off until this is filled in. Restart after:
#   systemctl restart binder.service
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# The path Binder is served under, when it is inside the Offdesk shell:
# BINDER_BASE=/binder
# The Bench link (plan 4) stays off until Bench issues Binder a token:
# BINDER_BENCH_TOKEN=
# BINDER_BENCH_CORE=   (a Bench core/ folder the binder user can read;
#                       Bench's /opt/agency is closed to other users)
# The Offdesk core's tools (core step 9a). Its token key, the same 64 hex digits as CORE_TOKEN_KEY in
# /etc/core/core.env; unset, the core's calls are refused:
# BINDER_CORE_TOKEN_KEY=
# Who may export or erase a person's data through the core (emails, separated by commas):
# BINDER_ADMINS=
# Events for the core (a lead moved or won); unset, none are kept or sent. The secret is the same value
# as HOOK_BINDER in /etc/core/core.env:
# BINDER_CORE_HOOK_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8780/api/hooks/binder
# BINDER_CORE_HOOK_SECRET=
